# ArabGT Backend — Project Memory

Django backend for the ArabGT platform (content/news, community, mobile API).

## Stack

- **Django 4.2 + Django REST Framework**; JWT auth (`simplejwt`) + `dj-rest-auth` with social login
- **PostgreSQL** (psycopg3) — pgbouncer config scripts at repo root
- **Redis + Celery** (+ celery-beat schedules, celery-results) — config in `core/celery.py`
- **modeltranslation** — content is bilingual (Arabic/English); model fields carry translated variants
- Media/storage: S3 via `django-storages` + `boto3`; rich text via CKEditor 5
- Push/SMS: OneSignal, Twilio; **Sentry SDK already integrated** (`sentry-sdk[django]`)
- Served with gunicorn + whitenoise; Docker + `docker-compose.yml` for local
- Timezone: `Asia/Amman`

## Layout

- `core/` — project package: `settings.py`, `celery.py`, shared `filters.py`, `utils` (custom DRF exception handler)
- Django apps: `adminpanel`, `analytics`, `api`, `authentication`, `base`, `cms`, `common`, `news`, `web` (+ `dashboard` work in progress)
- `env/` — local Python virtualenv (never touch); `env_varaibles/` and `.env*` files — secrets (**never read or print their contents**)
- `locale/` — translations; `docs/` — internal docs

## API conventions

- DRF with a **custom exception handler** (`core.utils.custom_exception_handler`) — keep error responses consistent with it
- Auth: real authentication first, with a **guest-user fallback** so unauthenticated requests still get a GuestUser identity — do not break this contract when touching auth
- New endpoints follow the existing app structure: serializer + view + url in the owning app; filters via `django-filter` / `core/filters.py`

## Bilingual content — IMPORTANT

Any new content model/field that users see must be registered for translation (modeltranslation) with Arabic and English variants. Check an existing model in `news/` for the pattern before adding fields.

## Red lines

- **Never run** `manage.py shell`, `dbshell`, `flush`, or any command against a non-local database. Local work targets the docker-compose database only
- **Never read** `.env*` files, `env_varaibles/`, or any credentials; never commit `dump.rdb`, generated dumps, or data files
- Migrations: `makemigrations` needs explicit approval; never edit an already-committed migration
- Celery tasks: changes to schedules (`celery-beat`) need explicit approval
- All work on branches; PRs only

## Commands

- Tests: `python manage.py test` (app-scoped: `python manage.py test news`)
- Checks: `python manage.py check`
- Local stack: `docker-compose up` (DB/Redis/worker — see `docker-compose.yml`)

## The MySQL news source — READ ONLY by design

The `news` app reads from a second database, `mysql_db` (external news source), routed by `core/routers.py` (`ReadOnlyRouter`):
- Reads for `news` models go to `mysql_db`; **writes are blocked** (router returns None) and migrations never run there
- Treat `news` models as **read-only mirrors of an external schema**: never add fields expecting local storage, never write to them, never run anything targeting `mysql_db`
- This is why `mysqlclient` is in requirements — it is live and required, not legacy

## Where new endpoints go (confirmed by Omar)

- Request is for the **mobile app** → the `api` app
- Request is for the **website** → the `web` app
- **Not sure which? STOP and ask Omar (or the requester) before writing** — never guess this boundary

## Code style (house convention — inferred and approved)

- No enforced formatter/linter yet — **match the style of the file you are editing** (quote style and layout vary per app; consistency within the file beats global rules)
- PEP8 baseline; serializers live in `serializers.py` per app (existing pattern)
- Candidate improvement for later: adopting ruff — decision pending

## Sentry (company org: `arabgt`)

- This service's projects, one per environment: `backend-dev`, `backend-qa`, `backend-staging`, `backend-prod`
- The mobile app has its own parallel set (`mobile-*`) — never mix them up when triaging
- **Read-only phase**: reading and analyzing issues is allowed; resolving/assigning/modifying anything in the company org is blocked by guard until the team approves writes

## Branch flow (confirmed by Omar)

- Daily work happens on **`dev`** — feature branches fork from `dev` and PRs target `dev`
- Promotion chain for releases: **`dev` → `qa` (testing) → `staging` → `production`**
- Never commit or PR directly to `qa`/`staging`/`production` — they receive promotions only

## Linear (company workspace: ArabGT)

- Every ticket for this repo goes to team **ArabGT**, project **"ArabGT Mobile App"** —
  the company's SINGLE shared Linear project (mobile + backend use the same one).
  Never create or target another project.
- Sentry-derived cards carry the `Sentry` label so they stay filterable among regular tickets.
- When AI finishes a fix (PR opened), move the ticket to the status named exactly
  **"In Review from AI"** — a human reviews after AI. If that status is ever missing,
  stop and ask; never substitute another status.

## Parallel work (multi-agent)

- Before dispatching parallel agents, map the files each task will touch.
  **No two agents may ever work on the same file** — overlapping tasks go to
  ONE agent sequentially instead.
- One git worktree + one branch per agent (`fix/<ticket-id>-<slug>` off `dev`).
  Never run parallel agents inside the main checkout.
- Each agent runs the FULL test suite before opening its PR. One task = one PR.
- When done, worktrees are removed (`git worktree remove`); branches live on in PRs.
